    "id": "ARBA00004173",
    "rule_id": "ARBA00004173",
    "description": "Assigns the UniProt subcellular location Mitochondrion (SL-0173) through 1,525 alternative condition sets built from CATH FunFam, InterPro and PANTHER signatures, each with a taxon condition. UniProt converts the location to GO:0005739 (mitochondrion) by GO_REF:0000044.",
    "review_summary": "Accuracy: all 1,525 live condition sets were screened with two UniProtKB queries each (2026-11-10): the Swiss-Prot entries that match the whole conjunction, and how many of them have the subcellular location Mitochondrion (SL-0173). In 1,513 sets every Swiss-Prot match has the location, in 11 some do not (sets 43, 209, 251, 365, 378, 456, 484, 549, 718, 1084, 1384) and set 1312 matches no Swiss-Prot entry. Three sets match plastid-encoded ATP synthase subunits curated to the chloroplast thylakoid membrane (set 251, 33 of 41 matches; set 456, 5 of 10; set 484, 7 of 12). Set 789 transfers a location that mouse and human SCP2 entries record for the short SCP2 isoform to full-length SCPx proteins, which the entries record as peroxisomal; Drosophila ScpX (Q9VJ43) receives Mitochondrion from it. Sets 1384, 1084 and 365 have minority matches located elsewhere. Precision on the 562,320 unreviewed entries the rule annotates was not measured. Meaning and parsimony: the rule assigns an organelle, and an organelle is an informative location in its own right. The 14 recorded sets select recognisable units (mitochondrially encoded cytochrome b, mitochondrial ABC transporters, HIG1 proteins, lipoyl synthase, ATP synthase subunits), and the complete screen shows that in 1,513 of 1,525 sets all reviewed matches are mitochondrial, so the sets do select what the label names. Where a unit has a known sub-compartment the rule stops at the organelle, but that is the level the rule is for, and only 47 of the 1,525 sets are covered by an ARBA rule for a mitochondrial sub-location. No set is subsumed by another. The defects are therefore the specific sets already identified, not the design of the rule.",
