    "description": "Assigns the UniProt keyword KW-0719 (Serine esterase) through 65 alternative condition sets built from InterPro, PANTHER and CATH FunFam signatures.",
    "review_summary": "Accuracy: all 65 live condition sets were screened with two UniProtKB queries each (2026-11-10): the Swiss-Prot entries that match the whole conjunction, and how many of them carry KW-0719 (Serine esterase). Every set matches at least one Swiss-Prot entry, and all 1,028 set-level matches carry the keyword. The screen shows that no set conflicts with curated keywords; it does not measure precision on the 83,725 unreviewed entries the rule annotates. Meaning and parsimony: the keyword is a class label (an ester hydrolase with a catalytic serine) and has no narrower keyword beneath it. Most sets select families of the alpha/beta-hydrolase fold for which the label is apt: cutinases, cholinesterases and type-B carboxylesterases, protein phosphatase methylesterase, BioH, FrsA, feruloyl and acetylxylan esterases, ABHD lipases. One unit sits less easily: six sets select HotDog-fold acyl-CoA thioesterases (ACOT7, ACOT8, ACOT9, ACOT11, ACOT12), which carry the keyword in Swiss-Prot although UniProt records Asn and Asp, not Ser, as the active site residues of human ACOT7. The clear parsimony defect is that the rule describes many families twice, once by InterPro signatures and once by a CATH FunFam, and at least eight of the FunFam sets match no UniProtKB entry that the InterPro set of the same family does not already match.",
