    "id": "ARBA00022679",
    "rule_id": "ARBA00022679",
    "description": "Assigns the UniProt keyword KW-0808 (Transferase) through 4,603 alternative condition sets built from InterPro, PANTHER and CATH FunFam signatures.",
    "review_summary": "Accuracy: 461 of the 4,603 live condition sets (every tenth set, starting with set 1) were screened with two UniProtKB queries each (2026-11-10): the Swiss-Prot entries that match the whole conjunction, and how many of them carry KW-0808 (Transferase). Every screened set matches at least one Swiss-Prot entry, and all 21,413 set-level matches carry the keyword. Ten sets are recorded with official signature names; the other screened sets were checked by count only. The screen shows no conflict with curated keywords; it does not measure precision on the 12,487,598 unreviewed entries the rule annotates. Meaning and parsimony: Transferase is the top-level enzyme class label, and the ten recorded sets each select a family of transferases (EPSP synthase, RNA polymerase, rRNA and tRNA methyltransferases, GlmU, NAD kinase, porphobilinogen deaminase, isopropylmalate synthase), so the label is true and is what the keyword is for. It is also the least informative statement available: UniProt's keyword hierarchy places 13 narrower keywords under it (Kinase, Methyltransferase, Glycosyltransferase, Nucleotidyltransferase, Acyltransferase and others), each with its own ARBA rule. Measured on all 4,603 sets, 2,661 (58%) are structurally covered by a condition set of one of those child-keyword rules, 1,196 of them by the Kinase rule ARBA00022777 alone. Keywords stack, so these sets do emit Transferase, but they restate, signature by signature, what the child rules already establish and what the keyword hierarchy implies.",
