    "rule_id": "ARBA00022912",
    "description": "Assigns the UniProt keyword KW-0904 (Protein phosphatase) through 214 alternative condition sets built from InterPro, PANTHER and CATH FunFam signatures.",
    "review_summary": "Accuracy: all 214 live condition sets were screened with two UniProtKB queries each (2026-11-10): the Swiss-Prot entries that match the whole conjunction, and how many of them carry KW-0904 (Protein phosphatase). Every set matches at least one Swiss-Prot entry and no set has a Swiss-Prot match without the keyword. The screen shows that no set conflicts with curated keywords; it does not measure precision on the 345,960 unreviewed entries the rule annotates. Meaning and parsimony: the 214 sets were sorted by their signatures into ten units, and the label fits them: the PPM/PP2C, PPP and protein-tyrosine-phosphatase superfamilies account for 162 sets, and the rest are CDC25, low-molecular-weight PTPs, SSU72, HAD-type CTD and EYA phosphatases, Rtr1/RPAP2, bacterial sensor kinase/phosphatases and a few small families. Protein phosphatase is a class label with no narrower keyword beneath it, so the rule is at the right level. Inside the PTP-fold unit some sets select enzymes whose main substrate is not a protein (myotubularins, PTPMT1, laforin, DSP4, the mRNA capping enzyme); they carry the keyword in Swiss-Prot and the rule copies it. The parsimony defect is specific: at least 14 single-FunFam sets match no UniProtKB entry that an InterPro-based set of the same rule does not already match, so they do no work.",
