    "description": "Assigns GO:0006661 (phosphatidylinositol biosynthetic process) from eleven alternative condition sets covering PI synthase, phosphoinositide kinases and phosphatases, and two lipid-transfer protein families.",
    "review_summary": "Accuracy: all eleven condition sets of the rule as served on 2026-11-10 were queried in UniProtKB and the reviewed matches of each were looked up in QuickGO. In every set the reviewed human, mouse, yeast or Arabidopsis members carry GO:0006661 (phosphatidylinositol biosynthetic process) on evidence other than this rule: Reactome TAS from the pathways R-HSA-1660499, R-HSA-1660516, R-HSA-1483248 and R-HSA-1483226, MGI IDA and IGI for the mouse PIP5K1 kinases (PMID:20622009), TIGR TAS for the plant PI 4-kinases, and IDA for PI synthase. So the rule reproduces what is in the annotation files. Set 11 is the exception: its FunFam is the FYVE zinc finger and it also matches Lst2 homologs, which have no phosphatase domain and carry the term only from this rule. Meaning and parsimony: reproducing annotations is not the same as saying something sensible. Only sets 8 and 9 select the enzyme that makes phosphatidylinositol, PI synthase. Sets 1, 2, 3, 5 and 11 select phosphoinositide phosphatases whose experimentally curated process is GO:0046856 (phosphatidylinositol dephosphorylation); sets 4 and 7 select phosphoinositide kinases, whose own term is GO:0046854 (phosphatidylinositol phosphate biosynthetic process); sets 6 and 10 select lipid transfer proteins curated to lipid transport terms. None of those terms is under GO:0006661, which has no descendants. The biosynthesis term reaches the non-synthase families through a pathway-membership mapping (Reactome \"Synthesis of PIPs\" pathways), so the rule puts \"PI biosynthesis\" on enzymes that remove phosphates from, or move, already made phosphoinositides. The rule is heterogeneous and should be split by what each family does.",
